window.docContents[89].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-two-fixes-finset-min-and-memoization":{"contents":"To deal with the second problem we compute the set of used colours once, and take\nthe smallest colour outside of this finite set. Naively this suggests we would need\nto compute the minimum over an infinite set, namely\nℕ \\ (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c, but that isn't the case.\n\nThe colours assigned by c to the earlier neighbours of v form the finite set\n(G.neighborLTFinset v).image c. If this set has k elements then at least one of\nthe k + 1 colours 0, ..., k is not in this set, and hence the smallest valid\ncolour can be chosen as the minimum of the finite set:\n{0, ..., k} \\ (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c. We can do this using Finset.min'.\n\nGiven a nonempty Finset of natural numbers (or of any type with a linear order),\nFinset.min' returns the smallest element of that Finset.\n\n#check Finset.min'     -- .. [LinearOrder α] → (s : Finset α) → s.Nonempty → α\n#check Finset.min'_le  -- If s : Finset α and x ∈ s then Finset.min' s ≤ x\n#check Finset.min'_mem -- If s : Finset α is nonempty then Finset.min' s ∈ s\n\n\nWe first formalize the statement that there exists a valid colour that is at most\n#((G.neighborLTFinset v).image c) (our k from the discussion above).\n\nlemma greedyChoiceMin_sdiff_nonempty (c : ℕ → ℕ) (v : ℕ) :\n    (range (#((G.neighborLTFinset v).image c) + 1)\n      \\ (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c).Nonempty :=\n  card_pos.1 <| (le_card_sdiff ..).trans' (by simp)\n\n\nWe can now define a new version of greedyChoice using Finset.min'.\n\nGiven any c : ℕ → ℕ and a vertex v : ℕ, G.greedyChoiceMin c v is the least\ncolour that is valid at v, computed by removing the used colours from\n{0, ..., k} (where k is the number of used colours) and taking the minimum./-- Given any c : ℕ → ℕ and a vertex v : ℕ, G.greedyChoiceMin c v is the least\ncolour that is valid at v, computed by removing the used colours from\n{0, ..., k} (where k is the number of used colours) and taking the minimum. -/\ndef greedyChoiceMin (c : ℕ → ℕ) (v : ℕ) : ℕ := Finset.min'\n  (range (#((G.neighborLTFinset v).image c) + 1) \\ (G.neighborLTFinset v).image c)\n    (G.greedyChoiceMin_sdiff_nonempty c v)\n\n\nOur two definitions of the greedy choice agree.\n\nlemma greedyChoice_eq_greedyChoiceMin (c : ℕ → ℕ) (v : ℕ) :\n    G.greedyChoice c v = G.greedyChoiceMin c v := by\n  simp only [greedyChoiceMin, greedyChoice]\n  apply le_antisymm\n  · -- greedyChoiceMin c v is a valid colour, so Nat.find returns at most it\n    exact Nat.find_min' _\n      (mem_sdiff.mp (min'_mem _ (G.greedyChoiceMin_sdiff_nonempty c v))).2\n  · -- greedyChoice c v lies in the finset that greedyChoiceMin c v minimizes\n    apply min'_le\n    rw [mem_sdiff, mem_range, Order.lt_add_one_iff]\n    exact ⟨G.greedyChoice_le_card_image c v,\n             Nat.find_spec (G.greedyChoice_exists c v)⟩\n\n\nIf we use greedyChoiceMin in place of greedyChoice in the definition of greedy,\neach call evaluates the partial colouring only once per earlier neighbour. So the\nrecurrence loses its (c(n) + 1) factor and becomes C(n) = 1 + ∑ C(m), summing\nover the earlier neighbours m of n. On the path this is linear, but on the\ncomplete graph it is still exponential because of the first problem:\nC(n) = 1 + C(0) + ⋯ + C(n - 1) = 2 ^ n.\n\nTo fix the first problem we build a list of colours [c 0, c 1, …, c (n - 1)] by\nordinary structural recursion on n, so that each colour is computed exactly once,\nand then look up the entry we need. This is a simple form of memoization.\n\nThe list of the first n greedy colours [c 0, c 1, …, c (n - 1)],\nbuilt by structural recursion./-- The list of the first n greedy colours [c 0, c 1, …, c (n - 1)],\nbuilt by structural recursion. -/\ndef greedyList : ℕ → List ℕ\n  | 0 => []\n  | n + 1 =>\n    let l := greedyList n\n    l ++ [G.greedyChoiceMin (fun m => l.getD m 42) n]\n\n\nThe list greedyList n has length n.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma length_greedyList (n : ℕ) : (G.greedyList n).length = n := by\n  induction n <;> simp_all [greedyList]\n\n\nWe will use List.getD to read off the colour of vertex n from\nG.greedyList (n + 1). As in greedy, the fallback value 42 is just filler: we\nonly ever look up entries that exist.\n\n#check List.getD\n\n\nThe memoized greedy colouring of G: build the list of the first n + 1\ncolours and look up the last entry./-- The memoized greedy colouring of G: build the list of the first n + 1\ncolours and look up the last entry. -/\ndef greedyMemo (n : ℕ) : ℕ := (G.greedyList (n + 1)).getD n 42\n\n\nWe now want to prove that this new version computes the same colours as greedy.\nThe list is correct: its m-th entry is greedy m. The induction is now the\nordinary one on the length of the list: extending the list by one entry\npreserves the earlier entries (List.getD_append), and the new entry is the\ncorrect greedy choice by greedyChoice_congr.\n\nlemma getD_greedyList {n m : ℕ} (hm : m < n) :\n    (G.greedyList n).getD m 42 = G.greedy m := by\n  induction n generalizing m with\n  | zero => grind\n  | succ n ih =>\n    simp only [greedyList]\n    rcases Nat.lt_succ_iff_lt_or_eq.mp hm with h | rfl\n    · rw [List.getD_append _ _ _ _ (by simpa using h)]\n      exact ih h\n    · rw [List.getD_append_right _ _ _ _ (by simp)]\n      simp only [length_greedyList, Nat.sub_self, List.getD_cons_zero]\n      rw [greedy_eq_greedyChoice, ← greedyChoice_eq_greedyChoiceMin]\n      exact G.greedyChoice_congr fun k hk => ih hk\n\n\nlemma greedyMemo_eq_greedy (n : ℕ) : G.greedyMemo n = G.greedy n :=\n  G.getD_greedyList (Nat.lt_succ_self n)\n\n\nNow each colour is computed exactly once, so colouring vertex n makes only n + 1\ncalls to greedyChoiceMin, whatever the graph. (You can check this by tracing\ngreedyList as we traced greedy above.) The number of calls is linear, although\nthe running time is not, since appending to a list and looking up its entries take\ntime proportional to its length.\n\nOf our two fixes, memoization is the decisive one: even paired with the original\nNat.find-based greedyChoice, it would remove the exponential blow-up, since\nevery colour would still be computed only once. Using greedyChoiceMin as well\nsaves recomputing the image for every candidate colour, which matters for larger\ngraphs.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*\u0009Making  greedy  Fast*","header":"7.8.6.2. Two Fixes:  Finset.min'  and Memoization","id":"/Colorings/#section-two-fixes-finset-min-and-memoization"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-neighbours-in-a-set":{"contents":"It is often useful to count only the neighbours of a vertex v that lie in a given\nfinite set s. We call this number G.degIn s v. We define it as the size of\ns.bipartiteAbove G.Adj v, which is Mathlib's name for {w ∈ s | G.Adj v w}. It\ncomes from Mathlib's double-counting library, so facts about degIn can be fed\nstraight into Mathlib's double-counting lemmas. We prove a few simple lemmas about\ndegIn, which we will use in Brooks' Theorem, for the\nMoore and Cage Bounds, and in the proof of\nFüredi's Stability Theorem.\n\nThe number of neighbours of v lying in s.section degIn\nopen Finset\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n\n/-- The number of neighbours of v lying in s. -/\ndef degIn (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (s : Finset V) (v : V) : ℕ :=\n    #(s.bipartiteAbove G.Adj v)\n\n\nG.degIn s v counts the vertices w ∈ s adjacent to v.\n\nlemma degIn_eq_card_filter (s : Finset V) (v : V) :\n    G.degIn s v = #{w ∈ s | G.Adj v w} := rfl\n\n\nThe set of neighbours of x lying in s is exactly G.neighborFinset x ∩ s.\n\nlemma filter_adj_eq_inter [DecidableEq V] (x : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)]\n    (s : Finset V) : {v ∈ s | G.Adj x v} = G.neighborFinset x ∩ s := by\n  ext v; simp [mem_filter, mem_inter, mem_neighborFinset, and_comm]\n\n\nSince G.Adj is symmetric, bipartiteBelow also counts the neighbours of v in\ns.\n\nlemma degIn_eq_card_bipartiteBelow (s : Finset V) (v : V) :\n    G.degIn s v = #(s.bipartiteBelow G.Adj v) := by\n  rw [degIn_eq_card_filter]\n  congr 1\n  ext; simp [adj_comm]\n\n\nThe number of neighbours of x in s is at most its degree.\n\nlemma degIn_le_degree (s : Finset V) (x : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)] :\n    G.degIn s x ≤ G.degree x := by\n  classical\n  rw [degIn_eq_card_filter, filter_adj_eq_inter, ← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n  exact card_le_card inter_subset_left\n\n\nIf x has at least as many neighbours in s as its degree, then every\nneighbour of x lies in s.\n\nlemma neighborFinset_subset_of_degree_le_degIn (x : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)]\n    (s : Finset V) (hle : G.degree x ≤ G.degIn s x) : G.neighborFinset x ⊆ s := by\n  classical\n  have : G.degIn s x = G.degree x := (degIn_le_degree s x).antisymm hle\n  rw [degIn_eq_card_filter, filter_adj_eq_inter,\n    ← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree] at this\n  have heq := eq_of_subset_of_card_le inter_subset_left (le_of_eq this.symm)\n  grind\n\n\nIf every neighbour of x lies in s, then x has G.degree x neighbours in\ns.\n\nlemma degIn_eq_degree {x : V} [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)] {s : Finset V}\n    (h : G.neighborFinset x ⊆ s) : G.degIn s x = G.degree x := by\n  classical\n  rw [degIn_eq_card_filter, filter_adj_eq_inter, inter_eq_left.mpr h,\n    card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n\n\nThe degIn of a vertex is monotone in the set.\n\nlemma degIn_mono {s t : Finset V} (h : s ⊆ t) (x : V) : G.degIn s x ≤ G.degIn t x :=\n  card_le_card (filter_subset_filter _ h)\n\n\nThe degIn of a vertex is additive over a disjoint union of sets.\n\nlemma degIn_union [DecidableEq V] (s t : Finset V) (x : V) (h : Disjoint s t) :\n    G.degIn (s ∪ t) x = G.degIn s x + G.degIn t x := by\n  simp only [degIn_eq_card_filter]\n  rw [filter_union,\n    card_union_of_disjoint (h.mono (filter_subset _ _) (filter_subset _ _))]\n\n\nIf some neighbour of x lies outside s, then x has strictly less than\nG.degree x neighbours in s.\n\nlemma degIn_lt_degree_of_adj_notMem {x y : V} [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)]\n    (s : Finset V) (hadj : G.Adj x y) (hy : y ∉ s) : G.degIn s x < G.degree x := by\n  classical\n  rw [degIn_eq_card_filter, filter_adj_eq_inter, ← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n  apply card_lt_card\n  rw [ssubset_iff_of_subset inter_subset_left]\n  exact ⟨y, (mem_neighborFinset _ _ _).mpr hadj, fun h => hy (mem_inter.mp h).2⟩\n\n\nIf every neighbour of x except possibly w lies in B, then x has at most\none neighbour outside B.\n\nlemma degree_le_degIn_add_one (B : Finset V) {x : V} [Fintype (G.neighborSet x)]\n    (w : V) (h : ∀ y, G.Adj x y → y ≠ w → y ∈ B) : G.degree x ≤ G.degIn B x + 1 := by\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n  classical\n  calc #(G.neighborFinset x) ≤ #(insert w {v ∈ B | G.Adj x v}) :=\n        card_le_card fun y hy => by\n          rw [mem_neighborFinset] at hy\n          by_cases hyw : y = w\n          · simp [hyw]\n          · simp [h y hy hyw, hy]\n    _ ≤ G.degIn B x + 1 := card_insert_le _ _\n\nend degIn\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Degrees","header":"4.3.6. Neighbours in a Set","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-neighbours-in-a-set"}});